{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011CffqTx4zWfXwS4w1mSzWa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change is a clean, like-for-like structural replacement that preserves the existing pipeline shape:\n\n- **Logic in the right unit.** The E.164 conversion rule lives entirely in `OwnerTelephones.toE164`, the designated single source of truth. `NormalizeOwnerTelephone` still does only \"convert-or-escalate,\" and `RejectDuplicateTelephone` still does only \"compare.\" The responsibilities didn't shift between classes; one method was swapped for another with the same role.\n- **No new duplication.** Both the normalizer and the duplicate-check go through `OwnerTelephones`, exactly as before, so the two paths still can't drift. The \"8–15 digits\" rule appearing both in the bean-validation `@Pattern` (guarding the persisted value) and in `toE164` (guarding the conversion) mirrors the pre-existing split (`^[0-9]{10}$` vs. the length-10 check), so it's consistent with the codebase's established design rather than something this diff introduces.\n- **No catch-all growth.** `OwnerTelephones` remains a tiny utility with one method; the function classes keep one public `service` method each, as the YAML wiring convention requires.\n- **Idiomatic.** The `null`-means-invalid contract on `toE164` with callers handling `null` (throw 400 / never-matches) is a reasonable, localized convention, and the Javadoc on every touched class was updated to describe the new rule — a maintainer here would find this natural."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":13388,"cache_read_input_tokens":11894,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":13388},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_managem